dotnetjq - commandline JSON processor [version 1.0.0; jq-1.8.2 compatible]

Usage:	dotnetjq [options] <jq filter> [file...]
	dotnetjq [options] --args <jq filter> [strings...]
	dotnetjq [options] --jsonargs <jq filter> [JSON_TEXTS...]

dotnetjq processes JSON inputs with jq-compatible filters and writes the results as JSON to standard output.

Command options:
  -n, --null-input          use `null` as the single input value;
  -R, --raw-input           read each line as string instead of JSON;
  -s, --slurp               read all inputs into an array and use it as one value;
  -c, --compact-output      compact instead of pretty-printed output;
  -r, --raw-output          output strings without escapes and quotes;
      --raw-output0         implies -r and outputs NUL after each output;
  -j, --join-output         implies -r and omits output newlines;
  -a, --ascii-output        escape non-ASCII output characters;
  -S, --sort-keys           sort object keys on output;
  -C, --color-output        colorize JSON output;
  -M, --monochrome-output   disable colored output;
      --tab                 use tabs for indentation;
      --indent n            use n spaces for indentation (maximum 7);
      --unbuffered          flush after each output;
      --stream              parse input in streaming fashion;
      --stream-errors       implies --stream and emits parse errors as arrays;
      --seq                 use application/json-seq framing and recovery;
  -f, --from-file           load the filter from a file;
  -L, --library-path dir    search modules from the directory;
      --arg name value      set $name to a string value;
      --argjson name value  set $name to a JSON value;
      --slurpfile name file set $name to an array of JSON values;
      --rawfile name file   set $name to the file's string contents;
      --args                collect remaining arguments as strings;
